| Release | Projects      | What we need first                                                  |
| ------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| R1      | P01, P02, P03 | Core DataFrame runner, CSV/Parquet fixtures, output grader          |
| R2      | P04, P05      | Multi-table fixtures, window tests, decimal correctness             |
| R3      | P06, P07      | Larger Parquet files, Delta environment, benchmark runner           |
| R4      | P08           | Delta MERGE, CDC batches, stateful test harness                     |
| R5      | P09, P10      | Streaming micro-batch harness, checkpoint isolation, recovery tests |

The first three projects can share our basic PySpark execution infrastructure. The intermediate and advanced projects introduce additional capabilities only when needed, keeping initial development manageable.

For every project, we should author a project manifest containing its dataset version, prerequisites, business rules, milestone definitions, starter files, expected artifacts, reference implementation, tests, and scoring rules.

## 7. Research-based decisions to keep

The research and your Spark/Delta notes point to three important design choices.

First, projects should be data engineering deliverables, not notebooks that only display a few rows. A learner should finish with transformed tables, reusable code, quality checks, and a clear explanation of what was built.

